iversity in  positions of power  i find that most people don't want to  address it i find that you have the ones  as i said who collectively of all colors  got together and have always fighted  anything whether it's apartheid civil  wars or anything else like that and as a  small group then you have the people who  are on the bad side who have an  intention and they feel that holding  back somebody is in some way or another  are going to benefit them it's the 80 in  the middle that are saying i'm not sure  this is real maybe i should do something  maybe i shouldn't and i find that that  is those are the ones that need the  education to understand why there has to  be a conscious effort to diversify in  various different ways of course gender  color creed and and color um but when  you do that you also get innovation if  you have the old boys or the old girls  come all thinking the same way you don't  get anybody to hack the system that's  exactly how fubu got here i didn't put  three sleeves on his shirt nobody was  addressing the fact that there was a  young market of primarily people of  color that love hip-hop music but also  once i created google there were people  of all colors who said we love this  culture and that's how it got to be so  big by getting with other people who  said you're right and i can help you  what you're doing and we're gonna both  make money and create change i love that  you said you didn't put three sleeves on  a shirt  i i love that and but but what so what  is it fear is that what it is for for  just to try and find diversity or  or it's just people i think that a lot  of people really they're too close to it  they don't or they're too close in their  bubble they don't believe it's happening  and listen i got to tell you the way  black ops news they came out was when i  reached out to a lot of organizations or  they reached out to me after the george  boy thing they said  i thought i was doing enough but i am  not clearly not doing enough but i don't  want to put lipstick on a pig i don't  want it to not be genuine where do i  start and that's where the conversation  starts to happen when you can say  where do i start is there a way that i  can help uh change this narrative on  what's going on in corporate america or  you know in the streets or in the world  you want to you want to you want to stop  making it twice as hard for an  african-american man and woman to move  into position or you know thinking the  two felonies are the same or people can  die over air fresheners or broken tail  lights or counterfeit bills uh and you  want to change that narrative and but  you have to make a conscious effort and  you have to educate yourself on why the  system systemic thing have been in place  and how you can change them what what's  the one that did you experience as you  were trying to build fubu when you were  sent to your current position where you  you encoura you you  um encountered a roadblock on this front  damon  you got one story i've been on that i've  been i've been pulled over plenty of  time but i've had guns put to my head by  cops and they've called me obviously the  n-word and you know i was only guilty of  having a bunch of t-shirts in my trunk  and trying to touch the stars that was  it period  um it is common in the black community  to to have that happen  um uh and many of my earlier partners  had been incarcerated for things they  didn't do and they would have to have  the innocence program uh you know fight  their case and get out ten years later  realize yeah and say you know i'm we're  sorry we realized you didn't do it now  here you go so it is it is very close to  me um  i i've seen it all all too often damon  john here on the rich eisen show the  second annual black entrepreneurs day  presented by chase for business will air  today exclusively on facebook and then  just for you um  damon i mean  if i had told you when you were just  starting fubu and you told my story on  m